---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const description =
  'The programs of the OpenAR Collective: open-source software, open knowledge, shared standards, training, and a neutral practitioner community.';
---

<Base title="Programs" description={description}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <p class="overline">Programs</p>
      <h1>What the Foundation builds and publishes</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The Foundation's software and published resources are free to access and use. Nothing it
        publishes requires membership, payment, or a commercial relationship of any kind.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="program prose" aria-labelledby="software">
      <h2 id="software">Open-source software</h2>
      <p>
        The Foundation's flagship program is a production-grade accounts receivable and collections
        platform that any organization can download, run, modify, and redistribute without a vendor
        relationship, a license fee, or permission from anyone. The software comprises two layers:
        the Wax application framework, a domain-agnostic foundation for auditable business
        applications, and the HiveAR accounts receivable platform built on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The software is released under the Apache License, Version 2.0. Source code is published
        publicly on <a href="https://github.com/OpenAR-Collective" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>, and
        outside contributions are accepted under the Developer Certificate of Origin. Compliance is
        a design principle rather than an add-on: tamper-evident audit trails, fine-grained access
        controls, and configurable compliance workflows are part of the core architecture.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Foundation is not a software vendor. It stewards the platform on behalf of the industry
        and the public, and it will never charge for it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="program prose" aria-labelledby="knowledge">
      <h2 id="knowledge">Open knowledge</h2>
      <p>
        The Foundation publishes open educational content, including guides, articles, white papers,
        and recorded webinars, addressing operational and compliance topics in accounts receivable.
        A library of plain-language compliance resources will cover the FDCPA, the FCRA, Regulation
        F, and key state-level consumer protection statutes, written to be accessible to
        practitioners who may lack dedicated legal or compliance staff.
      </p>
      <p class="notice">
        Compliance and regulatory resources published by the Foundation are educational reference
        materials only. They describe general regulatory frameworks and do not constitute legal
        advice, create an attorney-client relationship, or address the specific circumstances of any
        organization. Retain qualified legal counsel for guidance specific to your operations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="program prose" aria-labelledby="standards">
      <h2 id="standards">Shared standards</h2>
      <p>
        The Foundation develops shared technical and operational standards for the industry,
        including integration standards and published naming conventions for community
        contributions. Standards are developed in the open, with community input, and published
        freely so any vendor or agency can implement them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="program prose" aria-labelledby="training">
      <h2 id="training">Training</h2>
      <p>
        The Foundation delivers training through free webinars and virtual workshops open to the
        entire community, with recordings archived for asynchronous viewing. As the platform
        matures, a formal certification and training program will follow for the vendors and
        professionals who work with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Certification examinations, courses, and renewals may carry fees, which support the
        Foundation's programs. Fees and standards are uniform across applicants, and no applicant
        receives preferential treatment based on a relationship to the Foundation, its leadership,
        or its directors. Certification never gates the software: the platform is open source, and
        anyone may run, modify, and build on it without certification of any kind.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="program prose" aria-labelledby="community">
      <h2 id="community">Neutral community</h2>
      <p>
        The Foundation maintains a peer community, hosted on Discord, that is open to every
        practitioner in the industry: compliance officers, developers, operations managers,
        collectors, agency owners, attorneys, consumer advocates, academics, and regulators.
        Commercial solicitation is prohibited, there are no sponsored channels, and no one can buy
        visibility. The community is the incubator from which future programs emerge; the direction
        of those programs will be set by the practitioners who participate.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/join">Join the community</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .program {
    margin-top: 2.75rem;
  }

  .program h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .notice {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--c-text-soft);
  }
</style>
